---
import { byRecommendedRank } from '../../lib/seo';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import storesJson from '../../data/stores.json';
import type { Store } from '../../lib/types';
import { PROVINCES } from '../../lib/types';

const stores = storesJson as Store[];
const cityCount = new Set(stores.map((s) => s.citySlug)).size;

// Shops that list trade nights among what they offer — computed from
// stores.json rather than hand-typed, deduped to one shop per city so the
// three examples aren't all pulled from the same town. Sorted by rating
// (undefined last) then review count first, same tiebreak as our other
// guides, so this tracks the directory's live data.
const tradeNightAll = stores.filter((s) => s.services.some((v) => v.toLowerCase() === 'trade nights'));
const tradeNightCount = tradeNightAll.length;

const tradeNightStores: Store[] = [];
const seenCities = new Set<string>();
for (const s of [...tradeNightAll].sort(
  byRecommendedRank,
)) {
  if (seenCities.has(s.citySlug)) continue;
  seenCities.add(s.citySlug);
  tradeNightStores.push(s);
  if (tradeNightStores.length === 3) break;
}

const title = 'Your First Card Show: What to Expect';
const description =
  'A newcomer’s guide to attending a Canadian card show: what happens, how to handle cards politely, cash vs. card, spotting real deals, and trade-night culture.';

const ld: Record<string, unknown> = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: title,
  description,
  image: new URL('/images/guide-first-card-show.webp', Astro.site).href,
  datePublished: '2026-07-11',
  dateModified: '2026-07-11',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Sports Cards Near Me' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Sports Cards Near Me' },
};
---
<Base title={`${title} | Sports Cards Near Me`} description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(ld).replace(/</g, '\\u003c')} />

  <section class="relative mt-6 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-bord bg-panel">
    <img
      src="/images/guide-first-card-show.webp"
      srcset="/images/guide-first-card-show@900.webp 900w, /images/guide-first-card-show.webp 1600w"
      sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 1024px"
      alt=""
      class="absolute inset-0 h-full w-full object-cover opacity-50"
      loading="eager"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />
    <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-ink via-ink/60 to-ink/25"></div>
    <div class="relative px-6 py-10 md:px-12 md:py-14">
      <nav class="text-sm text-muted"><a href="/guides/" class="hover:text-paper">Guides</a> / {title}</nav>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-5xl md:text-7xl">{title}</h1>
      <div class="refractor-rule mt-4 w-28"></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="mt-10 max-w-prose space-y-6 text-paper/90">
    <p>
      A card show is just a room full of folding tables, each one rented by a different dealer, all set up for a day
      or a weekend in a community hall, arena concourse, or hotel ballroom. Most charge a small admission fee at the
      door, then walk the aisles browsing binders of loose "singles" (individual cards sold on their own, not in a
      pack or box), display cases of graded cards, and boxes of cheaper "commons" (the everyday base cards that make
      up most of any set) you're welcome to dig through. Nobody
      expects you to buy anything, and nobody's tracking how long you browse a table before moving on. If you've only
      ever bought cards online, the first show can feel like a lot at once — this guide walks through what actually
      happens, how to handle yourself once you're there, and how to find one near you.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-3xl">What to Bring</h2>
    <p>
      Pack light, but pack a few specific things. Cash in small bills is the single most useful thing in your pocket
      — more on why below. If you're hunting something specific, bring a written want list so you're not relying on
      memory table after table. A few penny sleeves and toploaders (rigid plastic holders that protect a card in
      transit) are worth tossing in a bag so anything you buy survives the trip home loose in your pocket. And if you
      collect anything valuable enough that condition really matters, a small magnifying loupe (a jeweler's-style
      handheld magnifier collectors use to check a card's surface up close) lets you check a card properly before you
      commit, rather than trusting the lighting on a folding table.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-3xl">Cash vs. Card</h2>
    <p>
      Most dealers at a Canadian card show still prefer cash, and plenty will only take cash — a card reader takes a
      processing cut out of a sale that's often already priced thin, so a lot of vendors would rather skip that
      entirely. Some tables do accept e-transfer or tap payment through a phone-based card reader, but don't assume
      it — ask before you're at the register mentally, not after. Don't count on the venue having an ATM on site —
      bring more cash than you think you'll need rather than counting on finding a machine once you're there.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-3xl">Handling Cards Politely</h2>
    <p>
      Card show etiquette is mostly common sense once someone spells it out. Ask before you pick anything up, even
      cards sitting loose in a box — it's the dealer's table and their merchandise. Hold cards by the edges only,
      since the oils on your fingers can leave marks on the surface over time, and that matters a lot more on a card
      someone's hoping to grade later. If a card's sealed in a hard plastic "toploader" or slabbed in a graded
      holder, don't pop it out yourself — ask the dealer to show it to you if you want a closer look. Put things back
      exactly where you found them in the binder or box, and don't crowd a table when someone else is already looking
      through it. None of this is complicated, but it's the difference between being welcomed back and being the
      person a dealer watches a little closer next time.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-3xl">Negotiating Without Being That Guy</h2>
    <p>
      Prices marked in a binder or on a box are usually a starting point, not a final number — most dealers expect
      some back-and-forth and won't be offended by a reasonable counteroffer. There's a real difference, though,
      between negotiating and lowballing: offering noticeably less than a card is worth, especially without asking
      any questions about it first, tends to end the conversation fast. You generally get more room to negotiate when
      you're buying several cards from the same dealer rather than haggling over one bargain-bin card for ten
      minutes — a small stack gives both of you something to work with. Decide your walk-away price before you're mid-negotiation
      standing at the table, and it's a lot easier to hold to it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-3xl">Spotting an Actual Deal</h2>
    <p>
      A deal isn't just a lower number than you expected — it's a price that's genuinely below what the card is
      trading for right now. Before you assume you've found one, check "comps" (comparable recent sold prices for
      the same or a very similar card — recently completed eBay listings are the easiest free source) rather than
      going on a hunch. Condition changes the math too: a card with a soft corner or slightly off centering can be
      worth meaningfully less than the same card in sharper shape, even though they look nearly identical at a
      glance across a table. And remember a "raw" card — meaning ungraded, sold on the dealer's word about its
      condition rather than a third party's — should generally sell for less than the same card already sealed in a
      graded holder, since the buyer is taking on the condition risk the grading company would otherwise absorb.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-3xl">Trade Nights and Show Culture</h2>
    <p>
      Shows aren't the only place collectors gather. Some local shops run informal "trade nights" — a regular evening
      set aside for collectors to bring cards and work out trades with each other, usually with less money changing
      hands than a show and more relationship-building. Trade nights show up in only {tradeNightCount} of our{' '}
      {stores.length} directory listings so far — including{' '}
      {tradeNightStores.map((s, i) => (
        <span>
          <a href={`/store/${s.slug}/`} class="text-prizm">{s.name}</a> in{' '}
          <a href={`/${PROVINCES[s.province].slug}/${s.citySlug}/`} class="text-prizm">{s.city}, {s.province}</a>
          {i < tradeNightStores.length - 1 ? (i === tradeNightStores.length - 2 ? ', and ' : ', ') : '.'}
        </span>
      ))}
      {' '}Plenty of shops run one without ever listing it anywhere online, so ask your local shop directly — it's a
      lower-pressure way to get a feel for trading before you ever set foot at a show table.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-3xl">Finding a Show Near You</h2>
    <p>
      Shows run on their own calendar — some annual, some monthly, some one-off — so the best move is checking what's
      actually coming up rather than guessing. Our{' '}
      <a href="/shows/" class="text-prizm">live show calendar</a> lists upcoming Canadian card shows with dates,
      venues, and admission, updated as new ones get added. If nothing's on the calendar near you right now, our
      directory of {stores.length} shops across {cityCount} Canadian cities is worth browsing in the meantime — plenty
      of local shops offer the same browsing, buying, and trading experience a show does, just on a Tuesday afternoon
      instead of a weekend.
    </p>

    <p class="text-sm text-muted">
      Know a show we're missing from the calendar? <a href="/suggest/" class="text-prizm">Suggest it</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
